  "index_test": "whole-animal-in-vivo",
  "index_test_evidence": "In the biomedical sciences, the ALS Therapy Development Institute observed no effectiveness of more than 100 potential drugs in a mouse model in which prior research reported effectiveness in slowing down disease, and eight of those compounds were tried and failed in clinical trials costing millions and involving thousands of participants ( Perrin, 2014 ). Of 12 replications of preclinical spinal cord injury research in the FORE-SCI program, only two clearly replicated the original findings – one under constrained conditions of the injury and the other much more weakly than the original ( Steward et al., 2012 ). And, in cancer biology and related fields, two drug companies (Bayer and Amgen) reported failures to replicate findings from promising studies that could have led to new therapies ( Prinz et al., 2011 ; Begley and Ellis, 2012 ). Their success rates (25% for the Bayer report, and 11% for the Amgen report) provided disquieting initial evidence that preclinical research may be much less replicable than recognized.",
  "decision": "include",
  "reason": "The study reports on the replicability of preclinical research, including studies using animal models (e.g., mouse models for ALS, preclinical spinal cord injury research, cancer biology studies). It quantifies the success rates of these animal model replications and compares them to original findings, which aligns with the criteria for including studies that report quantitative measures of agreement or concordance between animal results and human clinical results (or at least the translation of preclinical findings). The text explicitly mentions 'animal experiments' and compares their replication rates to 'non-animal experiments' (which are described as in vitro). The study provides quantitative data on replication success rates (e.g., 25% for Bayer, 11% for Amgen, 12% of preclinical spinal cord injury research replicating). This directly addresses the core requirement of quantitative comparison of animal model results."
